- Improved droplet formation model for mixer modelling (with the ability to tailor the model to varying organic and aqueous systems); 

- Improved droplet collision and coalescence model for settler modelling (with the ability to tailor the model to varying organic and aqueous systems); 

- Incorporation of mass transfer into CFD models for mixer-settlers and to a lesser extent pulsed columns.  This was based on a comprehensive literature review of mass transfer in SX that considered fundamentals, experimental measurements and previous work on modelling mass transfer.  A mass transfer sub-model for a copper system was built into a two-phase CFD model of the laboratory mixer and experimental measurements were used to validate the predicted results. Subsequent validation in the pilot scale was also undertaken and a range of physical and operating conditions were studied; 

- Flow patterns and detailed analysis around critical items such as impellers, baffles, chevrons, fences etc.  In particular, the flow patterns around and through settler furniture (e.g. Figure 4) were validated initially at the laboratory and pilot scale using an ultrasonic velocity profiling (UVP) system and later were validated at the full operational scale by measurements in operations (see below). 

With this improved modelling capability including ability to obtain detailed analysis around critical items such as impellers, baffles, chevrons, fences etc, its application to interrogate potential improvements in mixers and settlers was exploited. For mixers, a range of modifications were assessed included running simulations with an R320 impeller at different clearances, modelling and assessing the effect of upward and downward pumping with an auxiliary A310 impeller, shortened baffles, a centrally positioned R320 with draft tube and an axial flow-pump impeller.  Much of this modelling was run in parallel with physical modelling work and thus comparisons of modelled and observed behaviour were readily and routinely made.  The general approach involved separating the functions of the impeller which include pumping action, droplet formation and intimate mixing, and considering designs or modifications for each function independently; thus novel selections and arrangements of impellers continue to be considered.  However, generally the research was limited to considering the use of components that are considered readily available or relatively simple modifications. 

