###### **The Planned Future:** 

Despite the material development in our understanding and benefits already delivered over the past three projects, there remains more to do to help realise further benefits for the industry in general and sponsors in particular.  We therefore plan to continue our investigation with the support of sponsors as part of a proposed project ‘SXT3-Delivering Improved Performance’.  This project, which will focus more on physical rather than chemical aspects, will incorporate the following fundamental and applied objectives: 

- Consolidation, understanding of principles and guidelines (mostly settlers); 

- Refinement of physical and computational tools for use in design of settlers; 

- Further improvements and the development of guidelines for improved settler and settler internals design / engineering going forward; 

- Design and assess various settler furniture configurations; 

- Implement full-scale engineering settler furniture design changes to reduce the entrainment loss, most likely via 1:1 case studies, using the aforementioned guidelines. 

- Exploitation of CFD modelling opportunities and develop more quantitative predictions of settler performance; 

- Develop and refine instruments for laboratory and on-site investigation of SX phenomena; 

- Develop a generic database with data presented in a form readily usable by operators to assist with self-diagnosis of settler issues; 

- Continuing support for operators with on-site measurements, diagnosis and both testing and validation of improvements; 

- Focus on modelling, understanding and optimising mixer performance, e.g. development of a low shear, high efficiency pump, via application of the tools developed throughout these projects.  The emphasis here is on reducing fine droplet formation without compromising mass transfer or the pumping performance of the impeller. 

